86045417] [17592186129162 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129162 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129162 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129163 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1045 "Level Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186129326 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e669e123-e445-550d-980b-09ee4b440f81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130016 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130016 #edb/ref 1061 22162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130016 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5af3abd2-cf0a-5230-8fd4-5f285e096712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130016 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130017 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130017 #edb/ref 1061 22163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130017 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c4da688-00e6-5efe-81b1-0c1749b7c89f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130017 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130018 #edb/ref 1061 22164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130018 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27f830a8-2df2-594e-9bbe-3c8973052195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130018 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130019 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130019 #edb/ref 1061 22180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130019 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b58904dd-f0af-56ab-937b-9eafb5f01b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130019 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130370 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the following function: \n\n$$\nw = f(x,y,z) =x^2 +y^2+z^2\n$$\n\nSadly, it is impossible to plot this function because four spatial dimensions ($x,y,z,$ and $w$) are needed, and space as we know it is restricted to three dimensions. \n\nHowever, we can get an idea of how the function $f(x,y,z)$ behaves by plotting surfaces of the form\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3 \\, : \\,f(x,y,z)=c\\right\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is some value that is in the range of $f.$ These sets are called **level surfaces** because they can be visualized as surfaces in three-dimensional space, and are \"level\" in the sense that each surface corresponds to a constant value of $w.$\n\nFor this particular function, all level surfaces are spheres. For example, if we let $w = 4,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z^2 = 4,\n$$\n\nwhich is a sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/12/12de396a8460b929f656a44d701752e248180f926f22a5f5c3eeecf355e9f31a.png)\n\nFunctions with more than two input variables are sometimes called **hypersurfaces** because they can be thought of as surfaces that exist within a space whose dimension is greater than $3.$ For example, the function \n\n$$\nw = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\ncan be thought of as a sphere in four-dimensional space, and is sometimes called a **hypersphere.**\n\nAnother way of thinking about the level surfaces of this function is that they correspond to the intersection of the hypersphere $w=x^2+y^2+z^2$ and the **hyperplane** $w=c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130370 #edb/ref 1065 3818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130370 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0682086d-c964-557f-930b-8d2c1ebfd879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130370 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445828 #edb/ref 17592186045